# OpenVPN server env defaults. Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in.
# The two REQUIRED vars have no defaults on purpose: the entrypoint exits with a
# FATAL line and the container restart-loops until they are set.
#
# Users are `name:password` pairs, comma-separated. Entries are split on the
# FIRST colon, so a password may contain colons — but not a comma or a newline,
# which the format cannot express. Names use letters, digits and _ @ + - only.
OVPN_USERS=
# The public IP or DNS name clients dial. It becomes the server certificate's
# CN and SAN, and clients verify it (remote-cert-tls server), so it must match
# what the client puts in `remote`. Changing it on an existing state volume
# keeps the OLD certificate — delete the volume to regenerate, then reissue
# every client profile.
VPN_SERVER_ADDR=
# Address pool. Clients draw addresses from it in connection order (unlike the
# wg and awg nodes there is no deterministic per-peer mapping). Must NOT
# overlap the container's own subnet — the entrypoint refuses to boot if it
# does. Fourth pool: ipsec 10.10.10.0/24, wg 10.10.20.0/24, awg 10.10.30.0/24.
OVPN_POOL=10.10.40.0/24
# DNS pushed to clients via dhcp-option.
CLIENT_DNS=1.1.1.1
# Where decrypted client traffic goes: the vpndogs router's SOCKS in. Change
# these only if the router does not listen at its default address/port.
CHAIN_SOCKS_HOST=vpndogs-router
CHAIN_SOCKS_PORT=1070
#
# Client profiles carry NO password: `auth-user-pass` makes the client prompt
# on connect. The passwords live here on the server only; a profile is safe to
# hand to a phone, credentials are not. Issue one with:
#   docker exec vpndogs-ovpn-inbound entrypoint.sh client-conf <user> > <user>.ovpn